### `parseDigits` — verbatim (lines 50–72)

```js
export function parseDigits(str) {
  let value = parseInt(str, 10);
  if (isNaN(value)) {
    value = "";
    for (let i = 0; i < str.length; i++) {
      const code = str.charCodeAt(i);

      if (str[i].search(numberingSystems.hanidec) !== -1) {
        value += hanidecChars.indexOf(str[i]);
      } else {
        for (const key in numberingSystemsUTF16) {
          const [min, max] = numberingSystemsUTF16[key];
          if (code >= min && code <= max) {
            value += code - min;
          }
        }
      }
    }
    return parseInt(value, 10);
  } else {
    return value;
  }
}
```

### Return value with no parseable digits
**`NaN`** (a number, `Number.isNaN(...) === true`). Reason: `parseInt(str, 10)` is `NaN`, so the fallback branch runs; `value` starts as the empty string `""`, the loop appends nothing for non-digit characters, and the final `return parseInt("", 10)` yields `NaN`. Note it is `NaN`, **not** `undefined`/`null`/`0`.

Verified by executing the function (copied to /tmp, unmodified logic) with Node v10.24.1:
- `parseDigits("")` → NaN; `"abc"` → NaN; `"xyz"` → NaN; `"hello world"` → NaN; `"  "` → NaN; `"-"` → NaN; `"abc123"` → NaN (latn digits are not handled by the fallback loop).
- Non-latn digits do work: `"٣٤٥abc"` → 345, `"abc٣٤٥"` → 345, `"〇一"` → 1.

